

### 1. `IncrementTask.java` (The Runnable Implementation)

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class IncrementTask implements Runnable {

    private final String threadName;
    private final ReentrantLock lock;   // Lock is passed from outside
    private static int counter = 0;     // Shared counter (static for simplicity)

    public IncrementTask(String threadName, ReentrantLock lock) {
        this.threadName = threadName;
        this.lock = lock;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(threadName + " is trying to acquire the lock...");

        // Acquire the lock
        lock.lock();
        try {
            System.out.println(threadName + " has acquired the lock.");

            // Simulate work with multiple increments
            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
                increment();
                Thread.sleep(100); // Small delay to observe interleaving
            }

        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            System.out.println(threadName + " was interrupted.");
        } finally {
            // Always release the lock
            lock.unlock();
            System.out.println(threadName + " has released the lock.");
        }
    }

    private void increment() {
        // Demonstrates reentrancy - same thread acquires lock again
        lock.lock();
        try {
            counter++;
            System.out.println(threadName + " incremented counter to: " + counter);
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    // Getter for final result
    public static int getCounter() {
        return counter;
    }
}
```

### 2. `ReentrantLockDemo.java` (Main Class)

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class ReentrantLockDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Create a single shared ReentrantLock
        ReentrantLock sharedLock = new ReentrantLock();

        // Create Runnable tasks (each gets the same lock)
        IncrementTask task1 = new IncrementTask("Thread-1", sharedLock);
        IncrementTask task2 = new IncrementTask("Thread-2", sharedLock);
        IncrementTask task3 = new IncrementTask("Thread-3", sharedLock);

        // Create threads
        Thread t1 = new Thread(task1, "Thread-1");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(task2, "Thread-2");
        Thread t3 = new Thread(task3, "Thread-3");

        System.out.println("Starting threads with ReentrantLock...");

        // Start all threads
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t3.start();

        // Wait for all threads to complete
        try {
            t1.join();
            t2.join();
            t3.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        // Print final result
        System.out.println("\nAll threads completed.");
        System.out.println("Final counter value: " + IncrementTask.getCounter());
    }
}
```

### How to Run
1. Create two separate files:
   - `IncrementTask.java`
   - `ReentrantLockDemo.java`
2. Compile and run:
   ```bash
   javac *.java
   java ReentrantLockDemo
   ```

### Key Benefits of This Structure
- Clean separation of concerns
- `IncrementTask` is reusable
- Lock is injected via constructor (better for testing and dependency management)
- No inner classes
- Static counter with getter for clean access from main

### Expected Behavior
- Only one thread executes the critical section at a time.
- Same thread can re-acquire the lock in `increment()` without deadlock (reentrancy).
- Final counter should always be **9** (3 threads × 3 increments).

